Q: Symfony request object doesn&#39;t return any query or request (GET or POST) parameters A: <p>More detailed information about my comment.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Symfony 7.4</p>
</li>
<li><p>PHP 8.2</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Possible problem 1.</strong></p>
<p>The .htaccess should contain the following :</p>
<pre><code>&lt;IfModule mod_rewrite.c&gt;
    RewriteEngine On

# If the requested path is not a file or a directory, then redirect everything to index.php
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
    RewriteRule ^(.*)$ index.php [QSA,L]
&lt;/IfModule&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>After  <code>sudo systemctl restart apache2</code></p>
<p><strong>Possible problem 2.</strong></p>
<p>Symfony routing ignores the parameters.</p>
<p>All parameters (in the URL) are available via the query property of the Request object. This is a ParameterBag.</p>
<p><code>$request-&gt;query</code> - GET‑parameters</p>
<p><code>$request-&gt;request</code> - POST‑parameters (<code>POST</code> or <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> or <code>multipart/form-data</code>)</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

private RequestStack $requestStack;

public function __construct(RequestStack $requestStack)
{
    $this-&gt;requestStack = $requestStack;
}

$request = $this-&gt;requestStack-&gt;getCurrentRequest();

$expires = $request-&gt;query-&gt;get('expires');
$signature = $request-&gt;query-&gt;get('signature');
$token = $request-&gt;query-&gt;get('token');

$locale = $request-&gt;query-&gt;get('locale', en'); // if necessary

// Verify
if ($request-&gt;query-&gt;has('token') || !$token) {...}

// Сan get all the parameters as an array
$allParams = $request-&gt;query-&gt;all();
</code></pre>

Q: recursive_directory_iterator&#39;s skip_permission_denied option appears to be ignored on macOS? A: <p>The previous answer relies on exception handling, but exceptions are slow and cumbersome.</p>
<p>If you have exceptions disabled or don't want to rely on them like me, the best solution I found is to just do your own recursion 1 level at a time and check permissions before diving deeper.</p>
<p>Here's an example that iterates recursively for file &amp; folders with user level read permissions</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>
template &lt;typename Action_X&gt;
void recurseDirectory(std::filesystem::directory_entry const &amp;dir, Action_X &amp;&amp;action)
{
    if ( !dir.exists() )
    {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;cannot recurse directory '&quot; &lt;&lt; dir &lt;&lt; &quot;', folder does not exist\n&quot;;
        return;
    }
    std::filesystem::directory_iterator dirIter{dir};

    for (auto const &amp;dirEntry : dirIter)
    {
        std::filesystem::perms permissions = std::filesystem::status(dirEntry).permissions();
        if ((permissions &amp; std::filesystem::perms::owner_read) == std::filesystem::perms::none)
        { // Skip no read permissions
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;No read permissions for '&quot; &lt;&lt; dirEntry &lt;&lt; &quot;', Skipping..\n&quot;;
            continue;
        }

        // Call action for this entry
        action(dirEntry);

        if (dirEntry.is_directory())
        { // Recurse into subfolders
            recurseDirectory(dirEntry, std::forward&lt;Action_X&gt;(action));
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::filesystem::directory_entry testDir{&quot;folder/to/recurse&quot;};
recurseDirectory(testDir, [](std::filesystem::directory_entry const &amp;entry)
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; entry.path() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
});
</code></pre>
<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> If an exception is thrown for a different reason than read permissions, your application will obviously still crash, but it's easy enough to guard against additional problems</em></p>
<p><em>Tested on MacOS Sequoia 15.5, C++20, clang++ &amp; g++</em></p>
